wasm64 gets the generic ioctl encoding, the packed epoll event and the libc
crate's own statx definitions, which is the x86_64 Linux ABI the visorjs
kernel speaks on it and the musl the libc crate describes there.

  * ioctl/linux.rs selects its opcode constants from an explicit
    target_arch list with no wasm64 arm, so `consts` is configured out and
    the crate does not compile (`unresolved import consts`). The kernel
    encodes ioctls the asm-generic way, as x86_64 does.
  * event/epoll.rs packs `Event` for the libc backend on x86_64 only, and
    the backend casts `Event` to `libc::epoll_event`, which the libc
    crate's wasm64 arm declares packed (sys/epoll.h). Without the arm the
    two disagree on size (16 against 12) and every epoll_wait past the
    first event is read at the wrong offset, with no error.
  * backend/libc/c.rs re-exports linux-raw-sys's STATX_* for musl targets
    whose libc crate lacks them, and names loongarch64 as the musl target
    that has them. The libc crate's wasm64 arm is in the same recent-musl
    group as loongarch64 (its build.rs), so both sets are in scope and
    every STATX_* is an ambiguous glob import, a hard error.
  * backend/libc/fs/types.rs offers AtFlags::STATX_{SYNC_AS_STAT,
    FORCE_SYNC,DONT_SYNC} on glibc only, from the libc crate's
    AT_STATX_*, which that crate also defines for recent musl, wasm64
    included. The linux_raw backend every other Linux target takes has
    them, so crates call them unconditionally on target_os = "linux"
    (oxc_resolver's metadata does), and without the arm they do not
    compile.

No other target moves.

